charon.plugins.eap-gtc.backend = pam
	XAuth backend to be used for credential verification.

charon.plugins.eap-gtc.otp_helper
	Absolute executable path to the runtime Fortinet MFA helper (Linux only).

	Used only for the connection selected by _fortigate_mfa_connection_, after
	verified MSCHAPv2 continuation. There is no default helper or fallback to
	the stored EAP password on this path. The executable is invoked directly,
	without a shell, with **--ike-id** followed by the IKE_SA's numeric ID and
	**--connection** followed by its connection name. **--push-capable** is
	included only if the gateway advertised push in the validated continuation.

	A successful helper exits with status zero and writes a 4 to 8 digit ASCII
	code, optionally followed by LF or CRLF, to stdout. Alternatively, exactly
	**PUSH\n** is a private local decision marker accepted only when push was
	advertised; it becomes an empty EAP-GTC response, not text on the wire.
	Empty output, cancellation, invalid output or an unsuccessful exit fails
	authentication. Further GTC requests receive empty responses after push is
	selected, without invoking the helper again. Push remains selected for the
	entire attempt; GTC request text is ignored and never triggers an automatic
	switch to OTP. If gateway policy permits OTP after push denial or timeout,
	start a fresh manual attempt after the current attempt ends. The gateway's
	exact denial and expiry exchanges have not been validated.

	The helper has a 125 second deadline. Its stdin and stderr are redirected
	to /dev/null, stdout uses a private pipe, and other daemon descriptors are
	closed. The child resets its signal mask and SIGPIPE disposition before
	execution. The helper inherits the daemon's privileges and may communicate
	with a foreground broker to obtain the runtime decision. No secret is
	passed in its arguments. Helper mode fails closed on non-Linux platforms;
	ordinary EAP-GTC behavior is unchanged.

	The helper runs synchronously while its IKE_SA remains checked out and a
	daemon worker is occupied. Blocking VICI **list-sas** enumeration and
	termination of this SA can therefore wait for the helper, up to its
	125 second deadline. The helper and broker must not perform these blocking
	operations to obtain context or cancel. Use the initiation event's SA ID
	and connection name, then cancel through the helper/broker protocol.
	**list-sas --noblock** may omit the busy SA; omission is not proof that
	the SA no longer exists. An asynchronous credential interface would be
	needed to release the SA and worker while awaiting user input.
